Creating a Kubernetes cluster is a solved problem. What comes after — delivery,
secrets, certificates, tenancy, observability, backups — is not, and that is
where the week goes.

So the second cluster is a repository you fork:
[hcloud-k3s-platform-kit](https://github.com/sasin91/hcloud-k3s-platform-kit).
Every number below was measured over four build-and-teardown cycles on real
hardware.

::: note
**Part 1 of 2 of [Where to start with
self-hosting](/blog/running-things-on-your-own-hardware/).** Next: [What a game
world server actually needs](/blog/what-a-game-world-server-needs/)
:::

## What it is

- **OpenTofu** for the nodes, consuming
  [terraform-hcloud-kube-hetzner](https://github.com/mysticaltech/terraform-hcloud-kube-hetzner)
  as a pinned dependency. Not a fork of it — that project solves cluster
  creation and none of it is reimplemented.
- **Flux** for delivery, with the controller manifests committed rather than
  bootstrapped. No personal access token, nothing written back to your repo.
- **SOPS and age** for secrets. Exactly one secret in the whole system is
  applied by hand and cannot be encrypted.
- **Gateway API** for routing, with the platform owning the Gateway and tenants
  owning Routes.
- **VictoriaMetrics, VictoriaLogs, Tempo** behind an OpenTelemetry collector —
  which is the piece that makes traces worth having, because it stamps
  `k8s.pod.name`, `k8s.namespace.name` and `k8s.node.name` onto every span. An
  application knows what it *is*; it does not know where the scheduler put it.
- **A namespace per tenant**, generated with its quota, limits, network policy,
  disruption budget, backup job, pod security level and RBAC.

It ships no example tenant, deliberately. A workload occupying a namespace
demonstrates nothing the template does not already state.

## The price, measured

Three nodes in Falkenstein, one control plane and two agents, plus a load
balancer. Gross figures include 25% Danish VAT.

| Item | Type | €/month |
|---|---|---|
| Control plane × 1 | cpx22, 2 vCPU / 4 GB | 24.36 |
| Agents × 2 | cpx32, 4 vCPU / 8 GB | 88.72 |
| Load balancer | lb11 | 9.36 |
| **Total** | | **122.45** |

The kit's *default* shape is three control planes and two agents, for real
quorum: **€171.17**, before the autoscaler adds a node.

A managed control plane from the large providers is roughly €70–75 a month on
its own, before a single worker. But their workers cost multiples of Hetzner's
for the same specification, so you are not saving money by *avoiding* managed
Kubernetes at this size — you are saving it by being on Hetzner at all. Those
are different decisions and they get conflated constantly.

## What it uses before your code runs

Cluster at rest, running one static site.

| Namespace | Pods | CPU | Memory |
|---|---|---|---|
| observability | 19 | 141m | 2,858 Mi |
| flux-system | 4 | 9m | 596 Mi |
| kube-system | 11 | 33m | 580 Mi |
| cert-manager | 6 | 6m | 286 Mi |
| traefik | 2 | 4m | 150 Mi |
| system-upgrade | 1 | 1m | 56 Mi |
| **platform total** | 43 | **194m** | **4,526 Mi** |
| **the actual application** | 2 | 2m | **8 Mi** |

CPU is a rounding error — 194m of 9,400m allocatable, about 2%. Memory is what
you are buying, and two thirds of it is observability. The overhead is 566 times
the workload I measured it carrying.

That is also why the shape makes sense with several tenants and not with one:
the same 4.5 GiB carries your second tenant and your tenth. Size the nodes for
the platform first and budget about 5 GiB before anything of yours runs.

One number worth knowing before you scale out: the cluster carries **161,507
active time series** at this size, and two control-plane histograms —
`apiserver_request_duration_seconds_bucket` and
`etcd_request_duration_seconds_bucket` — are 27% of them, unqueried. That is the
default in every Kubernetes monitoring stack I have used. Dropping or
aggregating those buckets is the cheapest win available, and the kit leaves the
decision to you rather than silently making it.

## Where k3s actually shines

![Three options across a spectrum — three FreeBSD boxes, Docker Swarm, and the k3s platform kit — beneath two axes running in opposite directions: components acting on your behalf increase to the right while things you must write yourself decrease, and Swarm sits where they cross.](/images/k3s-platform-kit/spectrum.svg)

I also run [a production app on one FreeBSD box](/blog/athletos-freebsd/) for
about €7 a month, and I have been through the arithmetic on doing the same with
three. Put those boxes on identical hardware and the cost difference vanishes —
three cpx32 is €133.08, *more* than the cluster, because the cluster's control
plane is a smaller machine. **The saving is not the operating system. It is that
the FreeBSD boxes fit in 4 GB and the k3s nodes do not.** So the cost argument
against Kubernetes is really an observability argument wearing a costume: if you
would build metrics, logs and traces anyway, you have already paid.

Docker Swarm sits in the middle and is genuinely under-rated. It is a mode of
the daemon you already run, `docker stack deploy` takes a compose file, and it
gives you scheduling, rolling updates, rollback, overlay networking, secrets in
an encrypted raft log and mutual TLS between nodes — in about a dozen verbs.
Engine 29.7.1 shipped in July 2026 with Swarm fixes throughout the series, so
"Swarm is dead" is almost always the removed *Classic* Swarm being reported as
live. What it has no answer for is the namespace: no admission stage, no
extension mechanism, no per-tenant budget or policy layer.

Which is the whole thing k3s wins on. I once measured guarantees across a mature
multi-tenant cluster: those emitted by the path that *creates* a tenant — quota,
limits, network policy, disruption budget, pod security level, RBAC — were
present in **over ninety percent** of namespaces. The same guarantees written
down as recommendations were present in **none**. Not fewer. None.

The axis is not enforcement versus suggestion. It is *in the generator versus
not in the generator*, and Kubernetes is an unusually good generator target
because every guarantee is an object with a name, a namespace and a schema, and
something at the door checks it. You can build a jail generator — `jail.conf` is
text — and it is a weekend, not a research project. What is hard to reproduce is
a check that finds what **predates** the generator, and refusal at runtime
rather than in CI.

So: **under about ten services with one person authoring the isolation, the
simpler answer wins on every axis that matters.** What flips it is the second
author. One person holds the invariants in their head accurately; two people
hold two slightly different versions, and the divergence surfaces months later
as a customer seeing another customer's data.

## What is proven, and what is not

I distrust "production-ready", so here is the line I will actually defend.

Verified on live hardware: a three-node HA control plane forms with etcd; the
whole reconciliation chain converges from git with no manual intervention, 9 of
9 kustomizations and 8 of 8 Helm releases; a secret committed encrypted is
decrypted in-cluster and consumed; a generated tenant serves public traffic on a
Let's Encrypt certificate the system trust store validates; traces arrive
carrying pod, namespace and node; the hostname admission policy refuses another
tenant's hostname, and refuses a route that declares none.

Not verified: **a restore** — the backup job is generated with every tenant and
has never been restored from. **The autoscaler under real pressure** — the test
project's quota left no room. **A control plane surviving the loss of a member**
— three formed and were healthy; I never killed one to watch the other two carry
on.

That second list is the honest part. I would rather publish it than discover you
found it first.

## The part I would keep

A capable model will produce something like this kit in an afternoon, and most
of it will work — the shapes are conventional and the tools are the obvious
ones. What it will not produce is the list of things that **report success**.

Every server healthy, SSH working, and the orchestrator not running, because Git
on Windows rewrote a file to CRLF inside a module the build tool cloned on my
behalf, where my `.gitattributes` had no say; the SELinux policy compiled *on
the node* rejected the carriage return, and the visible symptom was an opaque
provisioner error two layers up. Or `tofu destroy` reporting success while an
autoscaler-created server kept running and kept billing, because destroy walks
its own state and the cluster creates things at runtime that never entered it —
and I had written "destroy is clean, zero residue" in the README, which was true
and useless, because those runs had never autoscaled anything.

Forty-nine of those are written down in the repository, each stated as a
mechanism with the failure it produced. That file is the part I would keep if I
had to throw the rest away.
